# MODULE.bazel.lock is ignored for now as per this recommendation:
# https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/20369
MODULE.bazel.lock

# Rust / Cargo (zlib-rs migration)
# Build output directory produced by `cargo build`/`cargo test` — never committed.
/target
# Cargo.lock IS tracked: the crate emits cdylib/staticlib distributable artifacts
# and the migration pins exact resolved dependency versions for reproducible,
# offline builds, so the lockfile is intentionally committed.

# cargo-fuzz (fuzz/ is a detached workspace; see .github/workflows/fuzz.yml).
# Build output and fuzzer-generated data are never committed; the fuzz targets,
# fuzz/Cargo.toml, and (once generated in CI) fuzz/Cargo.lock ARE tracked.
/fuzz/target
/fuzz/corpus
/fuzz/artifacts
/fuzz/coverage
# Reproducers libFuzzer writes when no -artifact_prefix is supplied. `cargo fuzz
# run` always passes one (pointing into /fuzz/artifacts above), but a target
# binary invoked DIRECTLY — the normal way to replay a single input, minimise a
# corpus by hand, or bisect a finding — defaults to `artifact_prefix='./'` and
# drops the file into whatever the current directory happens to be. Run one from
# the repository root over a corpus holding a crashing unit and the reproducer
# lands here as `./crash-<sha1>`, untracked and, without these five patterns,
# NOT ignored: it then shows up in `git status` and is one `git add -A` away from
# being committed. The names are libFuzzer's own fixed prefixes for its five
# finding classes. Anchored with a leading `/` so they match only at the
# repository root and cannot shadow a legitimately named file in a subdirectory.
# `fuzz/seeds/**` — the committed, read-only gate-opening seed inputs — is
# deliberately NOT ignored and is unaffected by these patterns.
/crash-*
/oom-*
/timeout-*
/leak-*
/slow-unit-*
